WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.639
In a world where clocks abound,

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:05.220
we constantly ask, "what time is it?"

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.900
but rarely do we stop consider, "what is time?"

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.400
In the July 25th Science News,

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.960
our staff writers tackle this question.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.609
They take a look at time from three

00:00:17.609 --> 00:00:20.391
completely different perspectives: body

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:24.570
brain and universe. When it comes to the

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:27.359
body's clock biologists ponder time from

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:29.340
the perspective of evolutionary history.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.520
They want to know how and why life-forms

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:34.500
acquire internal clocks that guide

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.620
everyday life. Molecular biology writer

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.230
Tina Hesman Saey explores the circadian

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.300
clocks built into nearly every one of

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.340
our cells and how they relate to the

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.200
most primitive clocks found in archaea

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.030
and algae. Our brains take on time is a

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.820
separate story. Neuroscience writer Laura

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.829
Sanders explores how the clocks in our

00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:57.960
heads help us make sense of the world

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.359
and what makes our perception time

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:02.579
appear to change as a consequence of our

00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:05.580
experiences. There are even some hints

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.350
about how faulty timekeeping in the

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.600
brain can factor into disorders such as

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.970
schizophrenia.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.040
Perhaps the most brain boggling

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.500
exploration time comes from physics

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.930
writer Andrew Grant. he grapples with

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.210
time in terms of grab and the grandeur

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:22.320
of the universe

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.470
why does time always run forward even

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:27.300
though the laws of physics should permit its tick

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.930
backward? A new simulation points to

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.120
gravity and suggest that the force may

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.520
allow the universe to have one past: the

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.700
big bang, and two futures.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.300
You can read about these explorations of time and more

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:52.520
at www.sciencenews.org/time.

